\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Integrable observables need not form an algebra}
\date{2026-10-25}

\p{Let #{Q} be positive self-adjoint on #{(E,h)} with #{n=\dim E>0}.
The Borel function #{F=e^{S_Q/2}} is integrable under the
[Gaussian law](fdip-0005), since
##{\int_E Fw_Q\,d\lambda_E
       =\int_E e^{-S_Q/2}\,d\lambda_E=Z_{Q/2}<\infty}
by [positive normalization](fdip-002T). But #{F^2=e^{S_Q}} is not
integrable: #{F^2w_Q=1}, and #{\lambda_E(E)=\infty}.
Indeed orthonormal cubes have masses #{(2R)^n} tending to infinity.}
\p{Thus actual integrable observables form a vector space but are not
closed under multiplication. The same counterexample works for their
almost-everywhere classes in #{L^1(\mu_Q)}. The
[polynomial algebra](fdip-002V) is an integrable algebra by \ref{fdip-002W}.
For #{n=0}, every real observable is constant on a singleton, so this
failure is absent.}
\p{This is a direct comparison of the two normalizers, using
\ref{fdip-002T} and the scalar ingredient in Keith Conrad's
\citet{Section 2, PDF pp. 1-2}{conradGaussianIntegral}; it also
retains #{e^{S_Q}} as a concrete Borel nonintegrable observable in
positive dimension.}
